Apply review 07 to the configuration schema

Answers:
- 1: no fifth layer. Team-scoped overrides live in the organisation
  file, selected by team, so precedence is unchanged.
- 2: any layer may tighten a floor, none may relax one. Validation
  enforces that floors only move in the stricter direction.
- 3: generalised provider instances. Every provider type may have
  several named instances with one default, selected per tier by
  ci.mapping. Replaces ciSecondary and covers several review providers
  and agent runtimes too.
- 4: defaults belong at both installation and organisation. Defaults
  are not floors.
- 5: budget shape deferred with Q16.
- 6: dry run uses fake or recording providers selected by
  configuration, not per-step declarations. Same mechanism workflow
  testing will need.

Issues:
- a: merge settings split. The organisation states what is allowed
  (methods, whether checks are required); the repository chooses its
  preferred method. A choice outside the allowed set is a validation
  error at run start, never a silent override. requireChecks is no
  longer repeated in feature-flow.yaml.
- b: os applies to the sandbox execution target only. A Windows tier
  delegated to CI must work in version 1; only a Windows sandbox
  requirement fails capability negotiation.
- c: retention becomes expectedRetention, checked at startup and
  warned on mismatch, not applied. It is a namespace setting owned by
  the platform team on managed Temporal.
- d: replica counts removed from engine configuration. Scaling belongs
  to the deployment; the engine names queues and capabilities.

Co-authored-by: Copilot <223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>
